# Lists & Recursion { .heading-no-number }

Lists are one of the most fundamental data structures in functional programming.
We'll learn how to create and process lists — including writing
our own recursive functions over them.

---

## Creating Lists

Square brackets create a list literal:

```koka
fun example1()
  val nums = [1, 2, 3, 4, 5]
  val words = ["hello", "world"]
  val empty : list<int> = []
  println(nums.length.show)   // 5
```

The standard library also has `list(lo, hi)` which generates `[lo, lo+1, ..., hi]`:

```koka
fun example2()
  println(list(1, 5).show)   // [1, 2, 3, 4, 5]
```

---

## The List Type

A `list<a>` is either:
- **`Nil`** — the empty list
- **`Cons(head, tail)`** — an element `head` of type `a` followed by a `tail : list<a>`

This recursive structure means lists are naturally processed with recursion.

---

## Pattern Matching on Lists

The `match` expression lets you branch on whether a list is empty or not:

```koka
fun describe(xs : list<int>) : string
  match xs
    Nil        -> "empty"
    Cons(x, _) -> "starts with " ++ x.show
```

This is _exhaustive_ — the compiler warns if you forget a case.

---

## Writing Recursive Functions

Let's write our own `sum` function that adds up all elements in a list:

```koka
fun my-sum( xs : list<int> ) : int
  match xs
    Nil         -> 0               // base case: empty list sums to 0
    Cons(x, rest) -> x + my-sum(rest)  // recursive case: add head to sum of tail

fun example3()
  println(my-sum([1, 2, 3, 4, 5]).show)   // 15
```

This pattern — matching on `Nil` (base case) and `Cons` (recursive case) — is the
standard way to process a list.

---

## Building Lists

To build a new list, use `Cons` to prepend an element:

```koka
fun my-reverse( xs : list<int> ) : list<int>
  fun go( remaining, acc )
    match remaining
      Nil         -> acc
      Cons(x, rest) -> go(rest, Cons(x, acc))
  go(xs, [])

fun example4()
  println(my-reverse([1, 2, 3]).show)   // [3, 2, 1]
```

The inner `go` function uses an _accumulator_ — a common technique for making
recursion tail-recursive (so it doesn't overflow the stack on long lists).

---

## `map`: Transforming Every Element

The `map` function applies a function to every element of a list:

```koka
fun example5()
  val doubled = [1, 2, 3, 4, 5].map(fn(x) x * 2)
  println(doubled.show)   // [2, 4, 6, 8, 10]
```

With a trailing lambda, this reads very naturally:

```koka
fun example6()
  val squares = list(1, 5).map fn(x)
    x * x
  println(squares.show)   // [1, 4, 9, 16, 25]
```

How would you implement `map` yourself? Here's one way:

```koka
fun my-map( xs : list<a>, f : a -> b ) : list<b>
  match xs
    Nil           -> []
    Cons(x, rest) -> Cons(f(x), my-map(rest, f))
```

---

## `filter`: Keeping Some Elements

`filter` keeps only the elements that satisfy a predicate:

```koka
fun example7()
  val evens = list(1, 10).filter(fn(x) x % 2 == 0)
  println(evens.show)   // [2, 4, 6, 8, 10]
```

---

## `foldl`: Reducing a List to a Value

`foldl` (_fold left_) combines all elements using a function and a starting value:

```koka
fun example8()
  val total = [1, 2, 3, 4, 5].foldl(0, fn(acc, x) acc + x)
  println(total.show)   // 15

  val product = [1, 2, 3, 4, 5].foldl(1, fn(acc, x) acc * x)
  println(product.show)  // 120
```

`foldl(init, f)` starts with `init` and applies `f(acc, element)` from left to right.
Most list-reduction operations can be expressed as a fold.

---

## Chaining List Operations

The real power comes from composing `map`, `filter`, and `fold` with dot notation:

```koka
fun example9()
  val result = list(1, 20)
                 .filter(fn(x) x % 2 == 0)   // keep evens: [2,4,...,20]
                 .map(fn(x) x * x)            // square them: [4,16,...,400]
                 .foldl(0, (+))               // sum: 1540
  println(result.show)
```

--- 

## Useful List Functions

The standard library has many list functions:

| Function | Description |
|----------|-------------|
| `xs.length` | Number of elements |
| `xs.head` | First element (returns `maybe<a>`) |
| `xs.tail` | All but first (returns `maybe<list<a>>`) |
| `xs.take(n)` | First `n` elements |
| `xs.drop(n)` | All but first `n` elements |
| `xs.reverse` | Reversed list |
| `xs.concat` | Flatten a `list<list<a>>` |
| `xs.zip(ys)` | Pair up elements: `list<(a,b)>` |
| `xs.foreach(f)` | Apply `f` to each element for its effect |
| `xs ++ ys` | Append two lists |

---

## Summary

In this section you learned:
- Creating lists with `[...]` and `list(lo, hi)`
- The `Nil`/`Cons` structure of lists
- Writing recursive functions with `match` on `Nil` and `Cons`
- The accumulator pattern for tail-recursive list processing
- `map`, `filter`, `foldl` for transforming and reducing lists
- Chaining operations with dot notation

